/**
 * @page topic_server_client Server Client State
 * @ingroup module_server
 * @brief Per-peer application state layered on the shared network runtime.
 *
 * `src/server/client.c` manages application state for each authenticated peer.
 * It is not the socket server: `network_server_t` owns listeners and connection
 * callbacks, while each `client_info_t` associates a connection with media,
 * terminal capabilities, queues, and render workers.
 *
 * @section server_client_open Open and negotiate
 *
 * `server_connection_open()` creates per-peer state and attaches it to the
 * `network_connection_t` context. The packet callback negotiates through the
 * shared transport, marks the connection ready, sends initial state, and
 * dispatches ACIP packets to server handlers.
 *
 * @section server_client_media Media workers
 *
 * Once a peer is ready, the server may create per-client video and audio render
 * workers. These workers personalize ASCII output and mixed audio; they do not
 * perform network reads or own connection lifecycle. Outbound packets go through
 * the connection transport and bounded packet queues.
 *
 * @section server_client_close Close and ownership
 *
 * Closing requests the network connection to stop and stops media workers, then
 * removes mixer sources, drains/destroys client queues, releases media buffers,
 * and clears application context. The network runtime releases the transport
 * after callbacks drain.
 *
 * @see topic_server
 * @see topic_network
 * @see topic_server_render
 */
